<<~ ahu slot=`#/sweep-reader-handback/cold-reader-lares-tasked-spirit-branch-sweep-handback`>>

! COLD READER: Lares (Tasked Spirit) — branch sweep handback
<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/sweep-reader-handback/findings-ranked-by-consequence`>>

!! Findings, ranked by consequence

1. ''The first delete command can make a partial sweep and then stop.'' [REACHED: lines 11–25, 129] Six names in `LOCAL_MERGED` do not descend into the current span or main: `feature/github-pages`, `feature/jekyll`, and `worktree-agent-a02ba494b7a8905b3`, `-a23a1f0db69506ac1`, `-a3ccbd391d4612f00`, `-ae821088d85a3d297`. The inventory records one ahead commit for each. Read-only `merge-base --is-ancestor` checks returned false for `feature/github-pages`, `feature/jekyll`, and `-a02ba494b7a8905b3`; the first has a gone upstream, and the sampled spirit has no upstream. `git branch -d` requires ancestry into HEAD or a merged upstream, so it will refuse at least those checked names. With several names in one invocation, Git may delete acceptable branches before returning failure; `set -e` then stops before `LOCAL_SQUASHED`, `LOCAL_HARVESTED`, and `ORIGIN`. The four spirit commits have `cherry_plus=0` in the scout inventory, which supports patch equivalence but does not make `-d` accept them. PR #5 and #7 mark the two feature branches merged, likewise without making their old tips ancestors.

2. ''A remote branch with exclusive history falls outside the stated deletion rule.'' [REACHED: lines 56–57] `origin/ci/ctl-gjd-5` stands five commits ahead of the span and neither span nor main contains its tip, per the inventory. A read-only `git log span..branch` showed those five commits, while `git cherry` marked all five patches equivalent (`-`). That offers a reason to consider deletion, but the operator's stated set names fully merged ancestry, squash-merged PR branches, four harvested July spirits, `feature/ftls-powers`, and superseded PR #46. This CI branch fits none of those named exceptions. Remove it from `ORIGIN` unless the operator explicitly extends the ruling to patch-equivalent CI history.

3. ''The remote tip check cannot protect against a later remote move.'' [INFERRED: lines 101, 105–116, 131] `git fetch` updates local tracking refs, and `check` compares those refs to nine-character scout IDs. A push by someone else after fetch and before line 131 can move a remote branch while the local tracking ref still passes. `git push origin --delete` carries no expected-old-tip lease and can then delete the new work. Pin full object IDs and use server-enforced expected-tip leases for every deletion. If the server supports it, request an atomic push for the remote batch; otherwise report partial success explicitly.

4. ''The preflight promises more than it checks.'' [INFERRED: lines 5–6, 100–122] It checks listed target tips and local worktree occupancy, but never checks the protected span or main tips, current repository identity, or remote URL. The span's local tracking tip now reads `381596346c02`, while the scout inventory captured `20728cdd6`; the script does not notice that base change. Recheck merge evidence against the freshly fetched bases and bind the run to `/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine` and the expected origin. `git fetch` happens before any prompt and writes Git state even when the operator answers `n`.

5. ''A successful `mapfile` can hide a failed list builder.'' [REACHED: lines 118–122] `mapfile -t A < <(names ...)` returns the status of `mapfile`, not the process substitution. A read-only Bash reproduction confirmed that `mapfile` returns success after a failing producer under `set -euo pipefail`. The `names` helpers also use `n` without `local`, though the current callers do not depend on its previous value. Build arrays with checked producer status, or count and validate every expected ref before prompting. `mapfile` also requires Bash 4 or newer, which the shebang alone does not guarantee on a stock macOS Bash.

6. ''Failure reporting will leave an uncertain partial state.'' [INFERRED: lines 126–136] A `read` EOF exits under `set -e` without the `REFUSED` explanation. A rejected `git branch -d` or `-D` may follow earlier successful deletions in its batch. A multi-ref `git push --delete` may delete some remote refs before reporting another ref's rejection; the final `core.bare` check and `Done` line then never run. Print a per-ref result or reconciliation command on failure, and do not present line 124 as a guarantee about the server's state at deletion time.

7. ''The script ends by suggesting deletion of a branch the operator wants kept.'' [REACHED: lines 93–96, 135–136] The arrays correctly omit `worktree-agent-a522be6155f677695`, and the worktree listing confirms it remains checked out. Yet the final message tells the operator how to remove that worktree and force-delete its branch. Remove those two lines; their suggestion conflicts with the explicit keep ruling.
<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/sweep-reader-handback/mechanics-read`>>

!! Mechanics read

[REACHED: lines 98–116, 126–133] `core.bare=false` currently passes; its first check runs before `git fetch`. The worktree parser found no listed local delete target checked out, while the kept `a522be6155f677695` branch does have a worktree. `grep -qxF ... && refuse` correctly allows an absent match under `set -e`; a match calls `refuse`. A moved tracked tip sets `moved=1` and refuses before deletion, while a missing ref prints `gone already` and silently leaves that target out. `[ "${#A[@]}" -gt 0 ] && ...` and its B/O counterparts safely skip empty arrays under Bash `set -e`; a minimal Bash check confirmed that a false left side of `&&` continues. The `read` prompt, however, comes after the fetch, and `n` refuses only then. The short-tip comparison detects movement in fetched local refs, subject to the remote race above. `git fetch` without pruning can also leave a stale remote-tracking ref for an already deleted server branch; the later push can fail after other deletes.
<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/sweep-reader-handback/scope-read`>>

!! Scope read

[REACHED: lines 11–96] The arrays hold 13 local `-d`, 15 local `-D` for merged PRs, four harvested July locals, and 35 origin refs. Comparing them with `branch-inventory.tsv` found no protected name in a delete array and no omitted inventory branch already marked as an ancestor of the span or main, after the explicit keep list. The four harvested locals appear exactly once. `feature/ftls-powers` and `--art-sigils-yin` appear in `ORIGIN`; the latter has PR #46 marked `CLOSED`, while #47 reads `MERGED`. The live `--w26-own-canon` and `--browser-secrets` lanes stay out, as do the four parked lanes, `--w26-floor`, `fold-prep-local`, backup, `github-pages`, main, and the span. Targeted short-tip reads matched the script for a harvested spirit, `--browser-custody`, and `--art-sigils-yin`. I did not independently reread all 67 target refs; the scout inventory supplies that broader comparison.
<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/sweep-reader-handback/opinion-and-verdict`>>

!! Opinion and verdict

''Not pono as written.'' First, repair `LOCAL_MERGED`: separate genuine ancestors from patch-equivalent or PR-merged old tips, and decide the latter's force deletion explicitly. Second, remove `ci/ctl-gjd-5` absent an expanded ruling. Third, protect remote deletion with expected-tip leases and a clear partial-failure report. Fourth, pin the repository, remote, and protected bases, then make list construction failures visible. Fifth, remove the closing suggestion to delete the kept worktree.

The order `-d` then `-D` then remote deletion sensibly places Git's conservative local check first and the shared remote last. It does not provide a transaction or rollback. Once a batch fails, the operator should reconcile actual refs before retrying rather than assume an untouched set.
<<~/ahu>>

<<~/ahu>>

